1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Lackerware decorated with designs formed in powdered gold or silver.

2
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Objects like this are widely used in Japan, from everyday tableware to precious utensils reserved for the tea ceremony.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Today we introduce this gorgeous traditional craft known as Machiae.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Over the centuries, Japanese lacquer workers developed lavish styles of decoration using powdered gold and silver to adorn their wear.

5
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,000
This technique known as Machiae is quite unique to Japan.

6
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:51,000
The first step is to trace the design motifs with lacquer onto the surface of the lacquered vessel.

7
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Then gold powder is carefully sprinkled onto the design so it adheres to the fresh lacquer.

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Hence the name, Macier literally,

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
means sprinkled picture.

10
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Looking at how Machiae is produced, there are three basic techniques.

11
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The method known as Hira or flat macchiay was developed from around 1,000 years ago.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The design is first traced with lacquer and then gold powder is scattered onto it.

13
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The technique is used.

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
The technique is used to create gradations of density in the shading.

15
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,000
A second technique is togidashi machiae.

16
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
A coat of lacquer is applied over a design in gold powder.

17
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
The surface is then smoothed down with charcoal.

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Because this creates a completely flat surface, this method is used when making trays.

19
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
The third basic technique is known as Taka Makkier.

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
as taka machiae, literally raised macchi.

21
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
The gold powder is sprinkled onto a thick layer of resin,

22
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
which creates a raised pattern.

23
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
The three-dimensional effect adds a new depth of beauty

24
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
to the gold's luster.

25
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
This lavish style of decoration was especially popular among the nobility.

26
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
The techniques for machiae have been known for over a thousand years.

27
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,000
This casket dates from the Heian period around 1,000 years ago.

28
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The design motif known as Genji Guruma represents cartwheels submerged in flowing water.

29
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
In the old days, the wheels of ox carts had to be soaked in water to prevent them from drying out and cracking.

30
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
This elegant design depicts a scene from ancient Kyoto.

31
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,000
For the Heian nobility, owning a gorgeous piece of Machia lacquer would have been considered a status symbol.

32
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:05,000
This machia screen decorated with Christian motifs was made for a European missionary during the late 16th century.

33
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:13,000
During this period, craftsmen also began to incorporate European design motifs.

34
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:19,000
The refined combination of black and gold proved popular with Europeans.

35
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,979
This is a writing box decorated by the writing box decorated by the

36
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
the renowned late 17th century artist Ogata Corrin.

37
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Machia is used to depict a scene inspired by an ancient literary work called The Tales of Isse.

38
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Iris flowers can be seen on the lid, with petals formed from Mother of Pearl.

39
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:54,000
It is intended to evoke a scene in which a man who has been exiled from Kyoto and has set out on a journey sees iris is blooming by a bridge.

40
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
is blooming by a bridge.

41
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
This makes him think of his wife, who he has had to leave behind.

42
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,000
The design continues on the inner surface of the box.

43
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
When the lid is removed, along with the ink stone inside,

44
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:14,000
a pattern of running watering Machiae becomes visible,

45
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
suggesting the river flowing underneath the bridge.

46
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Taking advantage of the box's structure, this scene from the tales of Issa is given a dramatic three-dimensional form.

47
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
form. This writing box is often considered to be the ultimate example of Machia craftsmanship.

48
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Each of the elaborate motifs adorning Machiae pieces has a deep significance.

49
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Even small everyday items may depict scenes from literature or reflect people's innermost hopes and prayers.

